Hi RK,

I may have released gmultmix too soon because there is no published paper to describe it. However, we hope to submit one soon. Although the model has broad applicability, it's probably best to think of it as a "single season" type of model in which the probability of an individual being available for detection (phi) is < 1, and the probability of detection given availability (p) is < 1. This allows one to relax the closure assumption, but it is not meant to estimate trends over multiple seasons per se. The model that I think you want was just published in Biometrics by Dail and Madsen (online only). We hope to include this in unmarked sometime soon, but it's not ready yet. In the meantime, you could try to adapt their R code, or you could use pcount
to either analyze each year separately or model year as a fixed
effect.

Back to gmultmix. Matt's comment is correct; gmultmix assumes a multinomial observation process, not a binomial process as would be the case with repeated counts nested within repeated counts. The binomial observation model may eventually be added.

users only need to specify "obsToY" and "piFun" when using multinomPois or gmultmix with data from a "non-standard" survey design, ie not with data from constant-interval removal sampling or double observer sampling. I will try to add more documentation regarding obsToY and piFun in the next release.

Hope this helps,
Richard

